{
  "$comment": "DECLARED CONSTITUTIONAL METADATA — the constitutional stage graph of the Universal Constitutional Lifecycle. This file is DATA. It is discovered, never imported: ucl_engine.py contains no stage id, no stage name and no owner path, and the declaration ucl-declaration.json contains none either. Both discover this manifest through a located manifest pattern, so a future constitutional stage becomes automatically discoverable by appending a node record here, or by adding a second manifest that the same pattern matches — with NO change to the engine, NO change to the declaration and NO architectural redesign. The graph is therefore OPEN: nothing in this file, in the declaration or in the engine records how many stages there are, and no field bounds the count. Ordinals ascend in steps of ten precisely so that a stage may be admitted BETWEEN two existing stages without renumbering any of them.",
  "$authority_comment": "AUTHORITY = NONE (DERIVED TRUTH). No node here creates a stage, an owner, an authority or a gate. Every node BINDS a constitutional stage to an owner the repository has already legislated and, where Repository Truth already declares an executable or governed counterpart, to that counterpart by reference. UCIC-001 owns the single deterministic lifecycle every capability follows; this manifest EXTENDS its stage coverage and replaces nothing. Where a node and a located instrument differ, the located instrument governs.",
  "$implementation_independence_comment": "Every node's owner and authority_owner is a DECLARED CONSTITUTIONAL INSTRUMENT — a constitution, an architecture, a determination, a register or a declared metadata file. The optional `evidence` list may cite implementation source, but evidence NEVER originates a node, an edge, an ordering or an obligation: it only corroborates one that declared metadata already established. The constitutional graph is therefore implementation-independent, and deleting every module named in every `evidence` list would not remove a single stage from the graph.",
